  "intro": "When a curve is defined implicitly by an equation in $x$ and $y$, differentiate both sides with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a function of $x$ (so every $y$ term picks up a factor of $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ by the chain rule). Then solve algebraically for $\\frac{dy}{dx}$. Evaluating at a point on the curve gives the slope of the tangent line there.",
  "items": [
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 1,
      "prompt": "Find $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ for $x^2+y^2=25$.",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "Differentiate both sides with respect to $x$: $2x+2y\\frac{dy}{dx}=0$.",
          "The $y^2$ term uses the chain rule, giving $2y\\frac{dy}{dx}$.",
          "Solve: $2y\\frac{dy}{dx}=-2x$, so $\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{x}{y}$."
        ],
        "answer": "$\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=-\\dfrac{x}{y}$"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 2,
      "prompt": "Find the slope of the tangent to $x^2+y^2=25$ at the point $(3,4)$.",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "From $\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{x}{y}$, substitute the point.",
          "$-\\frac{3}{4}$.",
          "The tangent at $(3,4)$ has slope $-\\frac{3}{4}$ (perpendicular to the radius)."
        ],
        "answer": "Slope $=-\\dfrac{3}{4}$"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 3,
      "prompt": "Find $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ for $xy+y^2=6$.",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "Differentiate: $\\frac{d}{dx}(xy)=y+x\\frac{dy}{dx}$ by the product rule; $\\frac{d}{dx}(y^2)=2y\\frac{dy}{dx}$.",
          "So $y+x\\frac{dy}{dx}+2y\\frac{dy}{dx}=0$.",
          "Group: $\\frac{dy}{dx}(x+2y)=-y$, so $\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{y}{x+2y}$."
        ],
        "answer": "$\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=-\\dfrac{y}{x+2y}$"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "practice",
      "id": 4,
      "prompt": "Find $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ for $x^2-y^2=9$.",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "Differentiate: $2x-2y\\frac{dy}{dx}=0$.",
          "Solve: $\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x}{y}$."
        ],
        "answer": "$\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\dfrac{x}{y}$"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "practice",
      "id": 5,
      "prompt": "Find $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ for $x^3+y^3=6xy$.",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "Differentiate: $3x^2+3y^2\\frac{dy}{dx}=6y+6x\\frac{dy}{dx}$.",
          "Group: $\\frac{dy}{dx}(3y^2-6x)=6y-3x^2$, so $\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{6y-3x^2}{3y^2-6x}=\\frac{2y-x^2}{y^2-2x}$."
        ],
        "answer": "$\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\dfrac{2y-x^2}{y^2-2x}$"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "practice",
      "id": 6,
      "prompt": "Find the slope of $y^2=4x$ at $(1,2)$.",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "Differentiate: $2y\\frac{dy}{dx}=4$, so $\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{2}{y}$.",
          "At $(1,2)$: $\\frac{2}{2}=1$."
        ],
        "answer": "Slope $=1$"
      }
